since the Gates Foundation was formed  back in 2000 by Microsoft co-founder  Bill Gates and his wife Melinda it has  paid more than forty five billion  dollars in grants supporting work in  more than 130 countries their work  addresses issues like vaccine delivery  family planning and emergency response  first on CBS this morning they're  revealing their 11th annual letter  highlighting they call it nine things  they didn't see coming  Bill and Melinda Gates our co-chairs and  trustees of the Gates Foundation we're  happy to say they're joining us a table  good to see you both so let's start with  this guy's because you've been wealthy  for a very long time whenever they say  Bill Gates and Melinda Gates they say  the world's richest people I wonder if  that moniker ever gets old but when did  you decide that listen we have all this  money we want to do something with it  Melinda well we were engaged to be  married and after we'd taken our first  trip to Africa actually a vacation we  were walking on the beach and we  committed at that point that we were  going to give the vast majority of the  resources for Microsoft back to society  just it was the right thing for us to do  bill you felt that - yeah we were  exploiting it and we've gotten more and  more engaged in it now it's our  full-time work so we were naive about  how to do it and how to have a big  impact but we knew that you know it was  amazing we have this fortune it was  gigantic we didn't think it would be  great to give lots of it to our children  and so and it became something that you  know we get to do together we're you  know partners in crime and you're  working very hard I want people to know  you're not sitting home you're both of  you were doing a lot of traveling you  just came back from Ethiopia bill but I  thought the letter was so fascinating  the thing that stuck out to me was about  data the importance of data I never  thought about that  yeah Lane why that's so important we  were surprised when we got into this  work having both come from the tech  sector that there was so little data  around philanthropy and we kept thinking  if we're gonna put another thousand  dollars into a project we have to know  whether the first ones worked whether  that projects worked and if we go call  in another government and so the data  can be sexist and data became very  important but that was the thing we  learned was that data that while we  think it's objective it's actually  sexist  we don't actually collect that much data  about women around  the world and so we don't know how to  make great investments on behalf of  women and so we've really highlighted  that issue in the last three years and  are working with our partners to collect  rate data event  well Africa has already come up twice in  this conversation oh you just came back  from Africa you talked about it being  the youngest continent and the real  growth potential there the the median  age is 18 in North America it is 35 why  is there potential that maybe the rest  of the world is not seeing and investing  in Africa and why is that so important  right now  well today Africa's only about 14% to  the world population but that's where  there's a large population growth and so  almost half the kids in the world we  will be born there by the end of the  century and so it's a very young  continent and the question of whether  those kids grow up with decent health  and decent education will determine  whether there's a lot of innovation and  they're stable and participating in the  world economy or if it's a source of  great instability and pandemics and so  investments we make now to partner with  Africa to help them out you know I think  can make a big difference and let me  piggyback on that one of the things you  argue and your letter is that national  that globalism can be a nationalist idea  explain that for people sure we know  that when you invest in people's health  and they start out healthy and they go  on to get a great education where they  live they'll grow up and thrive and  they'll participate in their own economy  and so there's huge potential there but  conversely if you don't they don't  thrive and then you get more instability  in the world less security disease we  know crosses borders we saw it with  Ebola so we need to be making those  investments so people can thrive where  they are and Bill let me ask you a  question about the domestic US you  learned something about anger by going  to prison tell us that story first  explain how the Bill and Melinda Gates  are sitting at prison what that was like  I was fascinated by that too John well  of course the US has more people in  prison than any other country and  understanding the stories of those  people and thinking about that in our  poverty work we there's  the Georgia prison that was so touching  then later I got a chance to in Chicago  meet with some young black men who were  in a counseling thing called becoming a  man and they super nice to me included  me in the session and it was a  discussion about anger and when's the  last time you got anger and how do you  channel that anger other times you don't  do that well I thought it was brilliant  and I was amazed how much that  counseling session was going to help put  those boys on the right track what was  your answer both of you to when was the  last time he became angry well I didn't  come up my I was talking about how  there's been more polio cases than we  hoped for this last year exact  experience last time for me was I I  would have said 'who's when I was in  Africa and I saw a baby die literally  dying needlessly on a warmer because the  mother hadn't made it to the health  clinic in time to give birth the village  didn't think she needed to go until too  late that made me angry the life we  could say race and sex play a big role  in this list in this letter this year  you talk about the mystery behind  premature births in particular among  african-american women in this country  african-american women are 49% more  likely to deliver their babies preterm  than white women one of the factors  could be racism explain that yes we  don't know all the factors but that is  on the rise as is maternal mortality in  the United States particularly under for  African American women and so we need to  look at all the factors is that the  health system is it the way they're  received there is it the stress in their  community is how we treat them is it  genetic we talk about selenium in here  we just don't know the answer but we  need to focus on that both in the US and  around the world around the world 10% of  babies are born prematurely and you know  if there's some thing in these puzzles  you've tried to solve over the last many  years that you've seen from outside the  United States  that you think hmm if we did things that  way in the United States and solving  these big seemingly insoluble problems  we might make some progress well the US  has by  for the world's most expensive health  care system and there are ways that we  could get better outcomes and be more  efficient we're more working in Africa  that total resources are like 1% per  person of what they are here in the US  and by focusing on vaccinations using  primary health care workers that don't  have to be doctors it's amazing what can  be done so that idea of task shifting  focusing on preventative disease that  that could work even in the US and the  thing that struck me guys was in the  letter you mentioned toilets you said  not sexy but toilets can save lives I'm  gonna leave that as a tease and say  people have to go to get the letter to  find out we as you point out bill they  haven't changed in a century and we all  need one thank you Bill and Melinda  Gates good to have you here

